==Latin==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|la|assis}}
* {{alt|la|𐆚||symbol}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|la|title=as|:bor<unc>|ett:-|text=+|tree=1}} Compare {{m|la|lībra}} and {{m|la|nummus}}, also loanwords. Original meaning was 'a rectangular bronze plaque weighing a pound'.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|as}}
* {{la-IPA|as.s|eccl=no}} ''(before a vowel)''
* Note: the word is singularly and unreliably attested in poetry,<ref>Anthologia Latina 741, 1 (1066, 1)</ref> but together with its derivatives usually measures long. It's more likely that it retained the geminate consonant before a vowel than that the vowel itself was lengthened. Most recent dictionaries give it as short, but EDL and certain other etymologists<ref>{{cite-journal|en|author=Brent Vine|title=&quot;Latin bēs/bessis &#39;two thirds of an as&#39;&quot;|work=Tavet Tat Satyam: Studies in Honor of Jared S. Klein on the Occasion of His Seventieth Birthday|year=2016|page=327|url=https://www.academia.edu/39081498/_Latin_b%C4%93s_bessis_two_thirds_of_an_as_|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260924154813/https://www.academia.edu/39081498/_Latin_b%C4%93s_bessis_two_thirds_of_an_as_|archivedate=24 September 2026}}</ref> as long; compare {{m|la|far}}.

===Noun===
{{la-noun|as/ass<3.I>|gen_pl=assium/assum|g=m}}

# {{lb|la|currency|historical}} [[#English|as]]{{ngd|, a [[Roman]] [[coin]] [[originally]] [[made]] of a [[Roman]] [[pound]] of [[bronze]] but [[later]] [[mint]]ed from 2, 1, and [[finally]] ½ [[Roman]] [[ounce]]s of [[copper]]}}
# {{lb|la|figurative}} [[penny]], [[copper]]{{ngd|, [[any]] [[similarly]] [[low-value]] [[coin]]}}
#* {{Q|la|Catullus|Carmina|5|1|thru=3|quote=Vivamus, mea Lesbia, atque amemus,<br>rumoresque senum severiorum<br>omnes unius aestimemus '''assis'''.|trans=Let us live, my Lesbia, and love, and account all the rumours of very grave old men as worth a single '''penny'''.}}
# {{synonym of|la|libra}}, [[Roman]] [[pound]]{{ngd|, a unit of weight}}
# [[unit]]{{ngd|, any [[undivided]] [[unit of measurement]]}}
# {{lb|la|with|{{m|la|ex}}}} [[estate]]{{ngd|, an [[undivided]] [[piece]] of [[property]]}}
# {{lb|la|figurative}} [[disc]]{{ngd|, [[any]] [[circular]] [[object]]}}
## {{lb|la|poetic}} {{synonym of|la|luna}}, [[moon]]
## [[circular]] [[flap]], [[circular]] [[valve]]
# {{lb|la|figurative}} [[slice]]{{ngd|, a [[piece]] of a [[circular]] [[object]]}}

====Usage notes====
It is especially significant as being the coin of least value in the Classical age. As such, it was often used in poetry as representative of the idea of worthlessness. This is seen in examples such as {{w|Catullus 5}}, where the poet mentions "valuing the opinions of old men at a single as", or {{w|Martial}}'s Epigram 103, where he mocks "{{m|la|asse}} {{m|la|cicer}} {{m|la|tepidum}} {{m|la|constant}} {{m|la|et}} {{m|la|asse}} {{m|la|Venus}}", meaning "you pay an as for warm pea soup, and an as for sex".

====Declension====
* The genitive plural is normally ''assium'', but ''assum'' is found in {{w|Varro}}.
{{la-ndecl|as/ass<3.I>|gen_pl=assium/assum}}

